## 6. Design Decisions (all SETTLED)

### [DD-COMP-SPEC-HOME] Spec home — a new `53-` doc extending [SPEC:CXC-CIC], not a section folded into 50

- decision: where the SVG-composition-source contract lives.
- chosen: author ONE cohesive `.lts` document — `corpora/platform-specs/53-composeux-cic-svg-composition.lts` ([SPEC:CXC-COMP]) — EXTENDING [SPEC:CXC-CIC] by reference (DEPENDS_ON), rather than folding it into spec 50. New node sub-family `CXC-COMP-*` (the registered `CXC-` root accepts it, [CONV-PREFIX]); DD family `DD-COMP-*`.
- rationale: the corpus convention is one-slice-per-numbered-doc; spec 50 is already a foundational shell doc, and folding the composition-derivation + mode-surface + STD-content/slot contract into it would bloat the foundational surface and mix "the shell exists" with "how composition is sourced". A separate 53 keeps [SPEC:CXC-CIC]'s node ids stable and mirrors the [DD-CAN-SPEC-HOME] precedent.
- alternatives: (a) fold into spec 50 as new sections (rejected: bloats the foundational shell doc, mixes concerns); (b) split into two docs (rejected: the facets are one decision, [DEC-SVG-COMPOSITION-SOURCE-OF-TRUTH], and share the by-reference + derivation machinery).
- invalidating_assumption: that the CIC family stays a multi-doc family (50 shell + satellites). If the architect prefers co-location in 50, the `CXC-COMP-*` ids fold to `CXC-CIC-*`.
- status: settled ([DEC-CXC-COMP-DDS-SETTLED])
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMP]

### [DD-COMP-MANIFEST-FORMAT] The composition manifest format — a derived JSON sidecar

- decision: what artifact the derivation emits and how the render consumes it.
- chosen: a machine-generated JSON SIDECAR co-located with the baked SVG bus asset (`bus://assets/cic/.../<name>.composition.json`), resolved by reference at render time; DERIVED from the SVG (never hand-edited); with in-memory derivation from the SVG as the fallback when the sidecar is absent ([CXC-COMP-MANIFEST]).
- rationale: JSON matches the deterministic extraction outputs the scan already emits and reads as a build artifact — its non-hand-editable feel is the point ("regenerate from the SVG, do not edit"). Bus-by-reference co-location means the render picks it up automatically; the constrained tier can consume a pre-baked order without a full SVG parse. In-memory-only re-parses every load and gives the embedded tier no pre-baked path — so it is the fallback, not the default.
- alternatives: (a) a hand-authored `.compose.yaml` (rejected: reintroduces a second hand-maintained source of z-order); (b) in-memory-only (rejected as default, kept as fallback); (c) embed the order inside the SVG as custom metadata (rejected: the SVG group ORDER already IS the source).
- invalidating_assumption: that the render benefits from a pre-baked order and that JSON sidecars fit the bus-asset resolve model. If the bus-asset store changes such that a sidecar is awkward, the in-memory derivation (already the fallback) becomes the sole path.
- status: settled ([DEC-CXC-COMP-DDS-SETTLED])
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMP]

### [DD-COMP-SLOT-NAMING] The `slot:` naming convention — grammar `slot:<widget>@<binding>` (the `@` delimiter)

- decision: the exact grammar of a `slot:` group label and how its binding key is scope-qualified. This is the one genuinely new build surface, so the convention is the load-bearing call.
- chosen (SETTLED, [DEC-CXC-COMP-SLOT-DELIMITER-AT]): `slot:<widget>@<binding>` with the `@` delimiter — `<widget>` is a registered reusable widget KIND ([CXC-COMP-WIDGET-KIND]) and `<binding>` is the bus binding key (the remainder after the first `@`, hyphens preserved), resolved under the screen's station+console scope ([CXC-CIC-DUTY-STATION]). A group whose label/id begins `slot:` is live; everything else (non-`ref:`) is static backdrop; the slot bbox is the widget rect; static children are the placeholder; styling comes from the inner SVG elements ([CXC-COMP-TEXT-STYLE]). Ground truth: `slot:radio@mode`, `slot:button@reboot`, `slot:button@shut-down`, `slot:text@firmware`, `slot:gauge@velocity`, `slot:graph@velocity-history`.
- rationale: the `@` delimiter is UNAMBIGUOUS even when the binding contains hyphens (`shut-down`, `velocity-history`) — the first-hyphen split was fragile for hyphenated bindings and would break the moment a widget kind contained a hyphen. `@` reads cleanly, keeps authoring in Inkscape (a label edit), and matches the committed base SVG's labels. Station+console scoping reuses [CXC-CIC-DUTY-STATION] so slots never cross-wire between stations; widget attributes (gauge min/max, units) come from a bound namespace, not the SVG label.
- change: "The slot grammar delimiter is `@` (`slot:<widget>@<binding>`), superseding the first-hyphen split (D1)."
- context_for_change:
    prior_state: "D1 of [DEC-CXC-COMP-DDS-SETTLED] chose `slot:<widget>-<binding>` — the widget kind the FIRST hyphen-delimited segment, the binding the remainder. The architect had recommended `@`; David chose first-hyphen."
    reasoning: "On authoring `colonial_base_ux.svg` David saw `@` is better: first-hyphen is fragile when the binding contains hyphens (`shut-down`). David reversed his own call to `@` ([DEC-CXC-COMP-SLOT-DELIMITER-AT])."
    rejected_alternative: "Keep first-hyphen and forbid hyphenated widget kinds (rejected: leaves the binding-side hyphen ambiguity; a latent fleet-wide rename)."
    counter_argument: "Reversing a settled D1 churns the convention."
    counter_response: "No screens had shipped against first-hyphen; the reversal is one guidance + one spec edit, not a rename of many files."
    invalidating_assumption: "That the widget kind and binding never both need unambiguous hyphens — the `@` delimiter removes the premise entirely."
    implication: "[CXC-COMP-SLOT] and every base/mode/screen slot label use `slot:<widget>@<binding>`."
- alternatives: (a) first-hyphen split (SUPERSEDED — the D1 choice, reversed by [DEC-CXC-COMP-SLOT-DELIMITER-AT]); (b) a `:` delimiter (`slot:gauge:velocity`) — rejected as visually colliding with the `slot:` prefix colon; (c) fully-qualified binding in the label — rejected as verbose; scope-qualification is the render's job.
- status: settled ([DEC-CXC-COMP-SLOT-DELIMITER-AT])
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMP]

### [DD-COMP-MIGRATION-SCOPE] The hand-maintained -> derived migration scope

- decision: how far the migration from the hand-maintained code z-order to the derived order reaches in this slice.
- chosen: migrate the CIC shell's layer assembly — `compose_projects/bsg/widgets/colonial_console.py` (`ColonialConsoleWidget(PixelStackContainer)` + the `compose_projects/bsg/widgets/cic/` support modules) — to populate its `pixel_stack` from the DERIVED layer order ([CXC-COMP-LAYER-ORDER]), removing the hand-maintained build-sequence index as the source of truth (the EXT-inversion bug source). The landed EXT layer-only-clipping fix (4c71eb2) is preserved as the correct BEHAVIOR; this migration removes the hand-maintained ORDER that made it fragile. In-memory derivation from the SVG is the only permitted fallback ([CXC-COMP-MANIFEST]); no parallel hardcoded index remains.
- rationale: the hand-maintained order IS the bug class; leaving it as a fallback preserves the drift the decision eliminates. Scoping to the CIC shell (the proven bug site + the STD-screen consumer) keeps the slice bounded.
- alternatives: (a) migrate every `pixel_stack` platform-wide (rejected: over-broad for one slice); (b) keep the hand-maintained order as a fallback (rejected: two sources of z-order is the drift the decision removes).
- invalidating_assumption: that the CIC shell is the only near-term consumer of SVG-sourced composition. If another surface adopts it, the migration extends (the derivation function is shared — adoption, not a rewrite).
- status: settled ([DEC-CXC-COMP-DDS-SETTLED])
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMP]

### [DD-COMP-OWNERSHIP] Base-vs-mode ownership is decided by element LOCATION — no per-mode override

- decision: how an element's shared-vs-mode-owned status is determined, and whether a mode may OVERRIDE a base element (specifically the status/firmware line).
- chosen (SETTLED, David 2026-08-04): ownership is decided by an element's authored LOCATION, not by any override flag — an element is SHARED iff it lives in the BASE SVG, MODE-OWNED iff it lives in the MODE SVG; there is NO per-mode-override mechanism ([CXC-COMP-MODE-SURFACE]). The status/firmware area (`slot:text@firmware`) is therefore BASE-SHARED and NOT mode-overridable ([CXC-COMP-BASE]). If an element must become mode-specific, it is REMOVED from the base and authored in each mode's SVG — not "overridden" in place.
- rationale: David's framing — "if [status] were meant to be mode-overridable, it would be cleaner to remove `slot:text@firmware` from the base and insert it in each mode document." Location-decides-ownership keeps the single-source discipline ([CXC-COMP-CREDO]): every element has exactly one authoring home, and there is no second override path to reason about or drift. An override mechanism would be a second source of the same element — the very drift the SVG-source model removes.
- alternatives: (a) base-shared with an optional per-mode override slot that supersedes when present (REJECTED: introduces a second source for one element — the drift the model exists to remove — and an override-resolution rule to maintain); (b) each mode owns its own status area, none in the base (REJECTED: duplicates the status widget across every mode and loses the shared shell). The chosen rule subsumes both cleanly: want it shared -> put it in the base; want it mode-specific -> author it in each mode SVG.
- invalidating_assumption: that no element needs to be present-in-base-yet-different-per-mode simultaneously. If such a case ever arises, it is handled by AUTHORING (move the element into each mode SVG), not by adding an override primitive — so the rule holds regardless.
- status: settled
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMP]

